    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d901">

                <pb facs="0843" n="831" sameAs="frus:frus1944v05/pg831"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.20 Defense(M)Turkey/996: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Turkey</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Steinhardt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Ankara</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-04-21T09:00:00+02:00">April 21, 1944—9
                            a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 7:46 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>717. Late yesterday afternoon, at the conclusion of a lengthy address to the
                    Grand National Assembly on the subject of chrome, the Minister for Foreign
                    Affairs announced to Parliament the Government’s decision to cease all shipments
                    of chrome to Germany and other Axis countries as of 7 o’clock this evening and
                    to forbid the further export of chrome from Turkey. Although I have not as yet
                    had an opportunity of talking with the Minister for Foreign Affairs since his
                    statement to Parliament, I assume that the prohibition of the further export of
                    chrome from Turkey will not affect the continued shipment by the British of the
                    very large quantities heretofore purchased by them and which at the rate of
                    shipment during the past 2 years will take at least 3 years to withdraw.</p>
                <p>My next numbered telegram<anchor n="26" xml:id="d901-f1"/> contains the
                    Minister’s statement to Parliament.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Steinhardt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
